WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:02.680
Film Courage: When did you decide on documentary
filmmaking?

00:00:02.680 --> 00:00:06.292
Your first film was a narrative or was it…there
wasn’t really a decision?

00:00:06.292 --> 00:00:10.070
There was an interest sparked for something?

00:00:10.070 --> 00:00:12.920
Matthew Miele, filmmaker (ALWAYS AT THE CARLYLE):
So my thinking behind it was I did a second

00:00:12.920 --> 00:00:19.870
a narrative called Eavesdrop and then that
had a very hard time getting out there.

00:00:19.870 --> 00:00:24.590
So I was very discourage because I was trying
to figure out well how am I going to tell

00:00:24.590 --> 00:00:25.980
the stories I want to tell?

00:00:25.980 --> 00:00:30.730
And then you have this huge mountain to climb
with raising money and the cast.

00:00:30.730 --> 00:00:34.069
You have a very small amount of time to get
it right with a feature because it’s only

00:00:34.069 --> 00:00:39.269
like a four or five-week on-set experience
and there are such long days.

00:00:39.269 --> 00:00:44.839
And I really was discouraged with the process
of storytelling and the feature and the feature

00:00:44.839 --> 00:00:45.839
narrative.

00:00:45.839 --> 00:00:48.899
However, from those two directing gigs (because
I’d gotten notice from some writing) because

00:00:48.899 --> 00:00:50.870
I had written scripts.

00:00:50.870 --> 00:00:54.199
So I came out here [Los Angeles] and I was
doing some writing and I got a manager and

00:00:54.199 --> 00:00:56.620
I was working with an agency.

00:00:56.620 --> 00:01:00.850
And I started working with a couple of high-profile
directors on a couple of scripts and I was

00:01:00.850 --> 00:01:03.609
helping to do a couple of things.

00:01:03.609 --> 00:01:07.670
But ultimately I got discouraged because with
that you sometimes are on turn around on a

00:01:07.670 --> 00:01:12.159
lot of things, you don’t get credited if
you work on something and you know, you’re

00:01:12.159 --> 00:01:14.490
in that development thing.

00:01:14.490 --> 00:01:17.710
And sometimes your work doesn’t really pay
off because you can be working on something

00:01:17.710 --> 00:01:20.090
for so long and never gets made.

00:01:20.090 --> 00:01:24.430
So then I had this frustration point where
I was like “I just want to tell my stories.

00:01:24.430 --> 00:01:28.040
How can I do this a lot easier in a sense?”

00:01:28.040 --> 00:01:33.579
And I’m a New Yorker in my heart and as
a child used to come into the city and see

00:01:33.579 --> 00:01:38.920
the windows at Bergdorf Goodman and a variety
of different storefronts.

00:01:38.920 --> 00:01:41.429
And I remember that as a kid and I used to
look up at these different windows and say

00:01:41.429 --> 00:01:46.700
“Wow, that’s like my first approach and
first encounter with storytelling and a three-act

00:01:46.700 --> 00:01:49.039
structure you could see within just one window.

00:01:49.039 --> 00:01:53.640
And I just absorbed that and I said to myself
“You know what?

00:01:53.640 --> 00:01:59.210
I’d love to do a film on Bergdorf’s because
that was my first encounter with this window

00:01:59.210 --> 00:02:00.380
(windows).

00:02:00.380 --> 00:02:04.890
I approached them and they said we don’t
allow filming and to do a whole feature would

00:02:04.890 --> 00:02:10.490
be crazy because you’d have to buy the store
out and film and it’s multi, multi millions

00:02:10.490 --> 00:02:13.030
to even think about it.

00:02:13.030 --> 00:02:14.440
But then I said “Well let me look at your
archive.

00:02:14.440 --> 00:02:16.560
Let me do a little more research on the place.”

00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:21.160
They showed me their archive and it was so
thin and I was like “Well, you don’t keep

00:02:21.160 --> 00:02:22.260
a formal archive?

00:02:22.260 --> 00:02:25.140
Well I’m surprised it’s not huge it’s
a hundred years old.

00:02:25.140 --> 00:02:26.140
And they said no.

00:02:26.140 --> 00:02:29.430
And I said well let’s do a project together.

00:02:29.430 --> 00:02:36.040
I’ll beef up your archive with interviews
and we’ll figure it out together.

00:02:36.040 --> 00:02:39.010
And I said “Let’s do a documentary”
And they said “Okay.”

00:02:39.010 --> 00:02:41.550
So for the next two years we were rolling
camera.

00:02:41.550 --> 00:02:47.480
And you can green light your project like
that without all the money you need to get

00:02:47.480 --> 00:02:48.480
it done.

00:02:48.480 --> 00:02:52.700
And also there’s this protracted period
where you can work on something for a longer

00:02:52.700 --> 00:02:58.650
period of time at your own pace and you don’t
have to have that really concentrated thing.

00:02:58.650 --> 00:03:04.130
And it was in 2011 when I started to think
about that and that’s when the doc started

00:03:04.130 --> 00:03:09.150
to get hot and get like this golden period
(which I still think we’re going through).

00:03:09.150 --> 00:03:15.660
And I feel like storytelling in a doc form
is a really refreshing approach if you really

00:03:15.660 --> 00:03:22.610
want to get your thing or your film made and
out there because you can literally for $1,000-$2,000

00:03:22.610 --> 00:03:26.000
bucks have a camera, start rolling and green
light your own project.

00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:30.430
It’s really about picking the right topic,
getting the right people that you want to

00:03:30.430 --> 00:03:33.800
interview or highlight and just making a compelling
story.

00:03:33.800 --> 00:03:38.220
And if you stitch that all together successfully
you can get something out there in a theatrical

00:03:38.220 --> 00:03:41.470
way just as much as a feature narrative is
these days.

00:03:41.470 --> 00:03:48.870
I mean my last four docs all went to theaters
and they were rated and you feel like you’re

00:03:48.870 --> 00:03:50.090
doing it and in it.

00:03:50.090 --> 00:03:55.360
But it was a byproduct and a result of my
frustration with the features side.

00:03:55.360 --> 00:04:03.620
So I’m kind of like in the doc world right
now (about to do two more that are already

00:04:03.620 --> 00:04:08.720
shot) but I’m entering back into the feature
world now because I got attached to direct

00:04:08.720 --> 00:04:09.950
this film on Norman Rockwell.

00:04:09.950 --> 00:04:12.590
It’s a biopic on that iconic artist.

00:04:12.590 --> 00:04:16.380
So I’m really excited about that so I’m
kind of like entering back in.

00:04:16.380 --> 00:04:21.030
But I feel better now because I’ve accomplished
what I’ve needed to in the doc world and

00:04:21.030 --> 00:04:26.160
I feel like with that experience I’ll bring
more to my directing prowess when I get on

00:04:26.160 --> 00:04:27.690
set and do that next thing.

